To enable real-time synchronization across devices for your web-based note-taking application, you can use WebSockets for minimal latency and efficient communication. Below is a code snippet that demonstrates the structure of a WebSocket-based implementation for real-time synchronization:

### Server-Side Code (Node.js with WebSocket)
```javascript
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });

let clients = new Set();

wss.on('connection', (ws) => {
  // Add the new client to the set
  clients.add(ws);

  console.log('Client connected');

  // Handle incoming messages
  ws.on('message', (message) => {
    console.log(`Received: ${message}`);

    // Broadcast the message to all connected clients
    for (let client of clients) {
      if (client !== ws && client.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
        client.send(message);
      }
    }
  });

  // Handle client disconnection
  ws.on('close', () => {
    console.log('Client disconnected');
    clients.delete(ws);
  });
});

console.log('WebSocket server is running on ws://localhost:8080');
```

### Client-Side Code (JavaScript)
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Real-Time Note-Taking</title>
</head>
<body>
  <textarea id="note" placeholder="Start typing your notes..."></textarea>
  <script>
    const ws = new WebSocket('ws://localhost:8080');
    const note = document.getElementById('note');

    // Send updates to the server when the user types
    note.addEventListener('input', () => {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ content: note.value }));
    });

    // Receive updates from the server
    ws.onmessage = (event) => {
      const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
      note.value = data.content;
    };

    ws.onopen = () => {
      console.log('Connected to WebSocket server');
    };

    ws.onclose = () => {
      console.log('Disconnected from WebSocket server');
    };

    ws.onerror = (error) => {
      console.error('WebSocket error:', error);
    };
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
1. **Server-Side**:
   - A WebSocket server is created using the `ws` library.
   - It listens for incoming connections and maintains a set of connected clients.
   - When a client sends a message (e.g., updated note content), the server broadcasts the message to all other connected clients.

2. **Client-Side**:
   - A WebSocket connection is established with the server.
   - The `textarea` element listens for user input and sends the updated content to the server.
   - When the server broadcasts updates, the client receives them and updates the `textarea` content accordingly.

### Key Features:
- **Real-Time Synchronization**: Changes made by one client are instantly reflected on all other connected clients.
- **Minimal Latency**: WebSockets provide a persistent connection, reducing the overhead of establishing new connections for each update.
- **Scalability**: The server can handle multiple clients simultaneously.

